    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Colonial rule placed a territory and its people under a distant metropole that ruled them as subjects, through its own officials (direct rule) or through local rulers it controlled (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>).</li><li>From Portugal’s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415 to the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, European empires spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and the Pacific through conquest, settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and trade.</li><li>Colonised peoples resisted throughout, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> to the liberation wars of the 20th century, and most colonies won independence between 1945 and 1975.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between them traced close to 40 per cent of the total length of the world’s international boundaries.</p></aside>
    </section>

